---
title: Add a language provider
description: Implement and register a provider, then connect its editor and task services.
section: How-to guides
order: 9
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use this guide to integrate another language with Rivet's project creation and editing workflows. You need a Rivet checkout, Lua, and knowledge of the language's file and build conventions. Use a bundled provider in `lua/rivet/languages/` and the `rivet.Provider` definitions in `lua/rivet/types.lua` as implementation references.

The provider produces specifications and file contents. The shared project service handles manifests, previews, conflict checks, and writes; see [Rivet's project model](/docs/project-model/).

## 1. Implement the model operations

Create a Lua module such as `my_rivet/languages/example.lua`. Return a table with all five functions:

| Function | Implement |
| --- | --- |
| `normalize(options)` | Validate inputs and return a specification without modifying the input. |
| `plan(options)` | Create the initial specification and file map. |
| `add_file(spec, options)` | Return an updated specification and added files. |
| `add_target(spec, options)` | Return an updated specification and target/build changes. |
| `add_dependency(spec, options)` | Return an updated specification and dependency/build changes. |

Keep these functions pure: no filesystem access, processes, or `vim` dependency. Validate identifiers, relative paths, and options. If an operation is unsupported, implement it with an error explaining the native alternative.

For `plan` and the three mutation functions, return this shape, filled with your language's real templates and metadata:

```lua
return {
  spec = {
    schema_version = 1,
    language = 'example',
    name = 'experiment',
    files = { 'src/main.example', 'build.recipe' },
  },
  files = {
    ['src/main.example'] = 'starter source\n',
    ['build.recipe'] = 'generated build instructions\n',
  },
  managed = {
    ['build.recipe'] = true,
  },
}
```

Keep every path relative to the project root and end text files with a newline. The project service creates `.rivet.json`; omit it from the provider's file map.

## 2. Define which files Rivet may regenerate

Mark generated build files with `managed[path] = true` in the initial plan and every mutation that updates them. Rivet records their generated contents and refuses to replace independently edited versions. Ordinary source-file additions must use new paths.

Add `ignore_rules(spec)` if the language needs generated ignore rules. For reusable creation profiles, list allowed fields in `profile_fields`; omit project names, identity, and machine paths. Add `default_name(folder)` to turn folder names into valid identifiers.

If you support template upgrades, implement pure `upgrade_files(spec)` returning supplementary configuration, build-support, and documentation templates. Exclude application sources, tests, local settings, and `.rivet.json`. [Project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) compare those templates with saved baselines and preserve local edits.

## 3. Register and inspect a plan

Make your provider module available on Lua's package path, then register it before use:

```lua
local model = require('rivet.model')
model.register('example', require('my_rivet.languages.example'))

assert(model.supports('example'))
local plan = model.plan({ language = 'example', name = 'experiment' })
assert(plan.spec.language == 'example')
assert(type(plan.files) == 'table')
```

Language IDs start with a lowercase letter and contain only lowercase letters, digits, or underscores. Registration cannot replace an existing provider and lasts only for that Lua process. The CLI does not discover a provider from `--language`; a custom launcher must register it before calling the CLI. Project manifests never load provider code automatically.

## 4. Connect the editor and native tasks

Implement `questions(kind, spec, context)` to expose `new`, `file`, `target`, and `dependency` questions in the wizard. Follow the bundled providers' step descriptors and supply a display `label`.

Register runtime services explicitly on the provider table:

```lua
services = {
  tasks = 'my_rivet.example_tasks',
  doctor = 'my_rivet.example_doctor',
  headers = 'my_rivet.example_headers',
  diagnostics = 'my_rivet.example_diagnostics',
}
```

Implement task discovery and argv generation in the task service. Use the CMake, native, or Lua task service as a reference for sequencing and executable discovery. The shared editor supplies output, cancellation, and quickfix integration. Doctor returns read-only diagnostics; the header service supplies language-specific comments; diagnostic parsing returns quickfix entries. Missing services report an error when requested.

If generated files need formatting, add `formatter(spec, filename, tools, context)` returning argv. The shared service runs it while preparing the preview. Creation and mutation calls supply a context with `root` and `existing`; upgrades may omit it. Use `local_files(spec, context)` for machine-local creation files; its context contains the resolved compiler when one was supplied.

## 5. Verify the integration

Add core tests for normalization, generated paths, mutation results, unsupported operations, and unchanged input specifications. Then exercise creation and previews in a temporary project, including collision refusal and edits to managed files.

Run the language's actual build, tests, and executable through its task service. Check diagnostic locations, cancellation, and recovery from a failed operation. For editor support, verify the questionnaire and final review with the repository's headless Neovim tests. A provider is ready when both its generated project and its supported Rivet workflows work end to end.
